WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.471
- I was so pumped.

00:00:01.471 --> 00:00:03.491
(woman laughs)

00:00:03.491 --> 00:00:04.324
Look at this.

00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:07.799
- [Woman] Ridiculous.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:08.936
- [Man] Greg is being interviewed.

00:00:08.936 --> 00:00:10.090
- Greg.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.630
My alternate ego who loves
television commercials

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.264
and is super passive.

00:00:15.264 --> 00:00:17.847
(techno music)

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.043
- How do you get to the moment of clarity?

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.450
- I think by never relenting
from the conversation

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.380
with myself about the
reality of the situation.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.640
Inevitably you'll get
to the moment of clarity

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.470
if you spend every minute recognizing

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.230
this is a fucking problem
and the quicker I address it

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.320
and understand it and respect it,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.420
the quicker I'm gonna get to the solution.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.140
Listen, this is why it's so scary.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.950
This is why I don't like that everybody

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.360
wants to be an entrepreneur.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.320
If you're listening to us right now

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.790
you're like, fuck, that sounds depressing,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.890
what's fun about that?

00:00:59.173 --> 00:01:00.006
It's the truth.

00:01:00.006 --> 00:01:00.839
- [Rachel] I know I always tell people,

00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:03.090
if you want to make money,
this is not the route.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.900
- To me, you can make money this way.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.760
If you don't want to deal with

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.920
unbelievable amounts of pressure,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.390
if you don't have the
stomach for the pain...

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.850
It's like being an athlete.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.503
If you wanna work out
every day and eat healthy,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.003
it seems so much more glamorous.

00:01:29.045 --> 00:01:29.878
- I think one of the things

00:01:29.878 --> 00:01:31.360
that I had learned in particular from you

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.330
is to be extremely self
aware as a founder.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.650
So when I started the business,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.690
I essentially had a chat with myself

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.220
and had myself acknowledge

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.690
what are the things I'm
the fucking best at?

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.880
And then what are the things
that I should not be doing?

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.790
And step one was to
surround myself with people

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.206
who could do all the things
that I am not great at.

00:01:53.206 --> 00:01:56.510
I'd say step three was
having the foresight

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.930
to know what type of
company I wanted to build,

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.143
and then find operators who'd done it.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.670
I don't need to reinvent
every aspect of the wheel.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.160
There are things that
Gary does every single day

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.360
that are tried and true to
building a great company

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.085
that I can learn from.

00:02:11.085 --> 00:02:12.770
So I would say that was a starting place

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.080
to identify who are the people

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.080
I should initially surround myself with.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.760
- Just to jump back before I move on,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.240
one thing I've been thinking a lot about

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.910
is building up self esteem and confidence.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.200
How do you find the right balance

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.450
between instilling confidence

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.450
versus creating delusion and entitlement?

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.696
I think it's an extremely fine--

00:02:29.696 --> 00:02:31.640
(audience laughs)

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.540
You like that one?

00:02:33.822 --> 00:02:35.040
I've been spending a lot of time on this,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.680
I'm just curious to get
your perspective on it.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.630
I think it's an extremely fine line and

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.100
I think that, for example,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.080
just being selfish for a minute,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:43.410
I think I've done too much of that

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.000
at Wine Library and VaynerMedia,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.090
which has then created an
undercurrent of entitlement

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.450
which takes a lot of time to then correct

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.250
and it's painful to start introducing

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.053
radical candor to massive optimism.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.653
How do you think about it?

00:02:59.653 --> 00:03:03.560
- Having humility and pride
in your voice at the same time

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.660
I think is what creates
authentic communication.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.380
So with my team, no one gets penalized

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.843
for making small mistakes,
because we're all human.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.430
I would say the big thing is

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.230
if things were happening over time

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.860
and we gave someone the benefit

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.020
to show growth in their role,

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.460
then we would sit down and talk about,

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.610
okay, how do we put you
on a performance review

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:25.790
that makes sense for the business

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.940
and help you achieve
these certain milestones?

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.810
So I think the fact that I have
such humility with my team,

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.470
like, Gary and I had a
board meeting yesterday.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.380
I left the board meeting,
I go back to my office,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.790
and of course my team wants to know

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.460
how the board meeting went.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.820
I told them exactly how it went.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.400
Because there's nothing to hide.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.500
So I told them the good, the bad,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.460
and having that level
of humility, I think,

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.070
is the balance with having
pride in your own voice.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.620
- Have you studied, you're
so early in your career,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.260
you're on this path, but
you are getting attention

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.550
and you're out and about.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.560
Have you started mentoring yourself?

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.380
Have you thought about what
that chapter looks like

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.710
or are you still very much focused on,

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.540
you're obviously still
early in the business,

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.070
focusing on getting the value

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.553
out of that relationship of mentorship?

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.530
- [Rachel] No.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.713
Similar to you, Gary,
I'm so self critical.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.330
I constantly, probably to a detriment,

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.340
every moment of the day,

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.430
I'm evaluating where the
weakness is in my business

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:28.680
in myself, what's coming up on my calendar

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.570
that I feel like I need help with.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.980
And I'll tell you,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.840
I feel like one of the
most underutilized words

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.960
in business right now is help.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.220
And I've actually put that into practice.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.053
- [Audience Member] So
you talked a little bit

00:04:39.053 --> 00:04:41.836
about correcting entitlement--

00:04:41.836 --> 00:04:42.860
(audience laughs)

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.380
And I'm kind of wondering for both of you,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.550
basically you could either
rule with love or fear.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.150
When you have team members who
are kind of underperforming,

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.850
how do you know when it's
time to do either of the two

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.273
or when to cut your losses?

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.360
- For me, fear is just
never a good strategy

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.793
even when it's not working.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.440
I'm flabbergasted by people's inability

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.160
to understand how much more
positivity and optimism,

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.060
even if you thought about

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.010
how Rachel articulated when
somebody was struggling,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.140
how do we put you on a plan

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.120
that works for the
business and works for you?

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.020
the thought that fear or negativity

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.550
is a good idea is ludicrous.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:30.350
There's always a great way to do it.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.060
I'll tell you, one of the
things I'm trying to do

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.060
a better job of is I'm
spending way more energy

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.850
on the way we exit somebody properly

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.830
than the way we onboard them properly

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.880
because I care so much
about the final taste.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.180
And when it's bad, I feel like

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.850
for all of us that are leaders here,

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:53.193
CEO, C Suite, what have
you, you work for them.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.610
And so you're in control and so for me,

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.170
I think there's a time
for critical feedback.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.250
To me, the cadence of
positive reinforcement,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.630
but you may want to look at this

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.160
and you think about your words carefully

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.500
and you wanna do it positive,

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.230
I do think there's a come
to Jesus moment, I think,

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:12.930
after giving positive reinforcement,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.270
but you may want to work on this.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:17.270
There is a time of
like, you probably suck.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:21.170
And that's okay, maybe we
suck, it takes two to tango,

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.990
but we need to figure this out
'cause nobody's winning here.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.450
My point of view is if
you're not able to be

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.020
the bigger person in every situation

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.220
when you have the leverage,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.080
you're weak and you're
creating vulnerability.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.770
- You said you invested in
Rachel and MikMak and LOLA.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:37.970
So I'd love to hear what's different

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:38.803
about what you're looking for

00:06:38.803 --> 00:06:40.370
and maybe what differences you see

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.613
in men versus women in fundraising.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.480
- This is something that
Rachel and Britt and...

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.460
I've done a lot of female...

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:52.360
What am I looking for?

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.733
I'm looking for good fucking businesses.

00:06:54.733 --> 00:06:56.819
(audience laughs)

00:06:56.819 --> 00:06:59.819
(audience applauds)

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.320
Obviously over the last half decade

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:06.060
as the conversation
properly has been focusing

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.823
on the numbers that aren't matching up,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.180
Rachel's always ragging on me
for not talking enough about

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.503
how much female foundership I've done.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.390
That is insane.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.470
I don't know that stat.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.810
I don't really pay attention
to much, believe it or not.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.063
That is unbelievably ridiculous.

00:07:32.292 --> 00:07:34.470
So I wrote the first check
into Birchbox, right?

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.090
And that was six or seven years ago

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.820
and I remember that meeting
like it was yesterday.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.870
We were in Starbucks in Midtown

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.630
and they were pitching

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.450
and they probably got about 60 words in

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.420
and I said I was in.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:50.810
And I remember them getting
super pumped and emotional.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.110
Pretty emotional.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:56.990
And I remember I just was like, awesome.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.675
I was pumped, too, but I was like...

00:07:58.675 --> 00:07:59.710
(audience laughs)

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.520
And they were just like,
we've gotten so many nos.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.120
We needed this yes.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.890
And I was like, cool,
I've seen that before,

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.320
I didn't think much of it.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.580
And it was super interesting,

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.330
it's probably around this time of year,

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.950
it was that first really
nice day in New York City

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:16.820
after the winter, you know that perfect,

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.770
I don't know if it was April
or May but it was that day,

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.780
I think we all know what day that was.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.230
And I was, ironically, less busy back then

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:24.780
so I could actually walk home,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:26.130
and I live on the Upper East Side.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:27.790
And in the 30 block walk,

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.313
I remembered somewhere
around halfway being like,

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:32.283
wait a minute.

00:08:34.078 --> 00:08:35.110
Is it possible...

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.310
The business was so
obvious to me, it was like,

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.640
we're gonna get samples and sell them.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.392
I was like, that's a
great fucking business.

00:08:41.392 --> 00:08:44.233
(audience laughs)

00:08:44.233 --> 00:08:46.670
And at the time it was very social media

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:47.840
and it was a different world,

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.440
maybe 'cause I came from a
retail background it clicked

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.800
but no question, it was a time

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.300
when it went through
my mind of, holy shit,

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.330
are they getting nos
because they're women?

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:01.000
And to be very frank, I'm
a pretty transparent dude,

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.930
I intuitively, this is not,
I'm not proud of this...

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.630
I wonder, and I don't
know if it's true or not,

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:11.050
but I wonder if I even subconsciously

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.990
recognized that there was gonna be

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:17.160
great opportunities for me
in female-founded businesses

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.990
if the market, back to
this two percent bullshit,

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:20.930
was gonna view it--

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.263
Like, to me, I don't
care if you're an alien.

00:09:26.344 --> 00:09:29.827
Only Patriot fans are the things
I usually don't invest in.

00:09:29.827 --> 00:09:32.660
(audience laughs)

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.670
It's utterly embarrassing to me.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:38.120
And not as a guy.

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.110
It's utterly embarrassing
to me as a businessperson

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.640
in the game of merit wins

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.900
that those stats exist.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.370
But I'll be very frank about this.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:51.090
and I'm a big, big, big fan from afar

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:55.473
of how Cindy Gallop
communicates this: fuck them.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.643
I'm being dead serious, fuck VCs.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.700
There's plenty of high net
worth individuals out there,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.770
there's plenty of other
ways to get dollars,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.433
or just make money from the beginning.

00:10:09.507 --> 00:10:12.340
I'm so thrilled that the internet is here

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.590
because, no, I mean
this, the mature internet

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:18.610
is unbelievably the middle layer.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:20.960
It is the layer that we build on.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.920
And that person isn't some 52 year old

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.750
Harvard Business School finance VC

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:31.000
that's making a subconscious
biased or biased decision.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.150
The internet is the middle,
and we don't have to play.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:37.650
And first of all, the whole VC ecosystem,

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.270
forget about men and women,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.610
is not an exciting game to begin with.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.160
Their model is predicated
on pushing 20 businesses

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.890
way too hard and way too fast

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:47.300
with the hope that one breaks through

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.730
and pays for the whole nut.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.700
It is so not founder-friendly
in the scheme of things.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.440
So to me, it's like, hearing that number,

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.680
where I really go in
my head is like, good!

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.050
Hopefully that makes all of
these badass women in here

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:03.000
think about alternative
ways to raise money.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.510
Because I don't think VCs
in their current state

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.333
is all that exciting.

00:11:09.809 --> 00:11:12.809
(audience applauds)

00:11:14.023 --> 00:11:15.783
- I have to say,

00:11:15.783 --> 00:11:19.520
I've never felt like Gary sees gender.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:21.630
You treat business as an athlete--

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.091
- [Gary] 'Cause I want to fucking win.

00:11:23.091 --> 00:11:25.350
(audience laughs)

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:26.693
It's insane to me.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:29.624
- He's the best.

00:11:29.624 --> 00:11:31.570
(audience laughs)

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:34.663
And there are other men out
there in the world like Gary.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:38.510
One of the reasons why I
asked Gary to be here is

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:40.640
I'm a true believer that feminism

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.220
will only reach its pinnacle

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.604
if men are a part of the conversation.

00:11:44.604 --> 00:11:48.296
(audience applause)

00:11:48.296 --> 00:11:49.296
- 100%.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.300
- [Rachel] Look for new
ways to raise money.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.840
I don't enjoy the VC ecosystem

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:59.633
and I have many strategic
investors at MikMak.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.881
- Question.

00:12:02.881 --> 00:12:05.381
(piano music)

